<p><b>Web scraping</b>, <b>web harvesting</b>, or <b>web data extraction</b=
> is <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Data_scraping" title=3D"Data =
scraping">data scraping</a> used for <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wi=
ki/Data_extraction" title=3D"Data extraction">extracting data</a> from <a h=
ref=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Website" title=3D"Website">websites</a=
>.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-1" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedi=
a.org/wiki/Web_scraping#cite_note-1"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>1=
<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> Web scraping software may d=
irectly access the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/World_Wide_Web"=
 title=3D"World Wide Web">World Wide Web</a> using the <a href=3D"https://e=
n.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hypertext_Transfer_Protocol" class=3D"mw-redirect" tit=
le=3D"Hypertext Transfer Protocol">Hypertext Transfer Protocol</a> or a web=
 browser. While web scraping can be done manually by a software user, the t=
erm typically refers to automated processes implemented using a <a href=3D"=
https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Internet_bot" title=3D"Internet bot">bot</a> =
or <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Web_crawler" title=3D"Web crawl=
er">web crawler</a>. It is a form of copying in which specific data is gath=
ered and copied from the web, typically into a central local <a href=3D"htt=
ps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Database" title=3D"Database">database</a> or <a =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spreadsheet" title=3D"Spreadsheet">sp=
readsheet</a>, for later <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Data_retr=
ieval" title=3D"Data retrieval">retrieval</a> or <a href=3D"https://en.wiki=
pedia.org/wiki/Data_analysis" title=3D"Data analysis">analysis</a>.
</p><p>Scraping a web page involves fetching it and then extracting data fr=
om it. Fetching is the downloading of a page (which a browser does when a u=
ser views a page). Therefore, web crawling is a main component of web scrap=
ing, to fetch pages for later processing. Having fetched, extraction can ta=
ke place. The content of a page may be <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/=
wiki/Parsing" title=3D"Parsing">parsed</a>, searched and reformatted, and i=
ts data copied into a spreadsheet or loaded into a database. Web scrapers t=
ypically take something out of a page, to make use of it for another purpos=
e somewhere else. An example would be finding and copying names and telepho=
ne numbers, companies and their URLs, or e-mail addresses to a list (contac=
t scraping). Another example is collecting competitors product prices for m=
arketing purposes, which can involve gathering large-scale pricing datasets=
 from e-commerce websites and analysing them using data science techniques =
such as trend analysis, predictive modelling, and competitive benchmarking.
</p><p><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Contact_scraping" title=3D"=
Contact scraping">Contact scraping</a> is a type of web scraping that is us=
ed as a component of applications used for <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.=
org/wiki/Web_indexing" title=3D"Web indexing">web indexing</a>, <a href=3D"=
https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Web_mining" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"We=
b mining">web mining</a> and <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Data_=
mining" title=3D"Data mining">data mining</a>, online price change monitori=
ng and <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Comparison_shopping_website=
" title=3D"Comparison shopping website">price comparison</a>, product revie=
w scraping (to watch the competition), gathering real estate listings, weat=
her data monitoring, <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Change_detect=
ion_and_notification" title=3D"Change detection and notification">website c=
hange detection</a>, research, tracking online presence and reputation, <a =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Web_mashup" class=3D"mw-redirect" tit=
le=3D"Web mashup">web mashup</a>, and <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w=
iki/Web_data_integration" title=3D"Web data integration">web data integrati=
on</a>.
</p><p><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Web_page" title=3D"Web page=
">Web pages</a> are built using text-based <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.=
org/wiki/Markup_languages" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Markup languages"=
>markup languages</a> (<a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/HTML" title=
=3D"HTML">HTML</a> and <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/XHTML" titl=
e=3D"XHTML">XHTML</a>), and frequently contain a wealth of useful data in t=
ext form. However, most web pages are designed for human <a href=3D"https:/=
/en.wikipedia.org/wiki/End-user_(computer_science)" class=3D"mw-redirect" t=
itle=3D"End-user (computer science)">end-users</a> and not for ease of auto=
mated use. As a result, specialized tools and software have been developed =
to facilitate the scraping of web pages. Web scraping applications include =
<a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Market_research" title=3D"Market r=
esearch">market research</a>, price comparison, content monitoring and <a h=
ref=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence" title=3D"Arti=
ficial intelligence">artificial intelligence</a>.  Businesses rely on web s=
craping services to efficiently gather and utilize this data.
</p><p>Newer forms of web scraping involve monitoring <a href=3D"https://en=
.wikipedia.org/wiki/Data_feed" title=3D"Data feed">data feeds</a> from <a h=
ref=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Web_servers" class=3D"mw-redirect" tit=
le=3D"Web servers">web servers</a>.  For example, <a href=3D"https://en.wik=
ipedia.org/wiki/JSON" title=3D"JSON">JSON</a> is commonly used as a transpo=
rt mechanism between the client and the web server.
</p><p>There are methods that some websites use to prevent web scraping, su=
ch as detecting and disallowing bots from crawling (viewing) their pages. I=
n response, web scraping systems use techniques involving <a href=3D"https:=
//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Document_Object_Model" title=3D"Document Object Mod=
el">DOM</a> parsing, <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Computer_visi=
on" title=3D"Computer vision">computer vision</a> and <a href=3D"https://en=
.wikipedia.org/wiki/Natural_language_processing" title=3D"Natural language =
processing">natural language processing</a> to simulate human-like browsing=
 to enable gathering web page content for offline parsing.
</p>
<meta property=3D"mw:PageProp/toc">
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id=3D"History">History</h2><span =
class=3D"mw-editsection"><span class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DWeb_scraping&amp;actio=
n=3Dedit&amp;section=3D1" title=3D"Edit section: History"><span>edit</span>=
</a><span class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<p>After the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_the_World_=
Wide_Web" title=3D"History of the World Wide Web">birth of the World Wide W=
eb</a> in 1989, the first web robot,<sup id=3D"cite_ref-2" class=3D"referen=
ce"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Web_scraping#cite_note-2"><spa=
n class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>2<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a>=
</sup> <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/World_Wide_Web_Wanderer" ti=
tle=3D"World Wide Web Wanderer">World Wide Web Wanderer</a>, was created in=
 June 1993, which was intended only to measure the size of the web.
</p><p>In December 1993, the first crawler-based web search engine, <a href=
=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/JumpStation" title=3D"JumpStation">JumpSt=
ation</a>, was launched.<sup class=3D"noprint Inline-Template Template-Fact=
" style=3D"white-space:nowrap;">[<i><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wik=
i/Wikipedia:Citation_needed" title=3D"Wikipedia:Citation needed"><span titl=
e=3D"This claim needs references to reliable sources. (January 2026)">citat=
ion needed</span></a></i>]</sup> As there were fewer websites available on =
the web, search engines at that time used to rely on human administrators t=
o collect and format links. In comparison, Jump Station was the first WWW s=
earch engine to rely on a web robot.
</p><p>In 2000, the first Web <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/API"=
 title=3D"API">API</a> and <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/API" ti=
tle=3D"API">API</a> crawler were created. In 2000, <a href=3D"https://en.wi=
kipedia.org/wiki/Salesforce.com" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Salesforce.=
com">Salesforce</a> and <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/EBay" titl=
e=3D"EBay">eBay</a> launched their own API, with which programmers could ac=
cess and download some of the data available to the public.<sup id=3D"cite_=
ref-3" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Web_scr=
aping#cite_note-3"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>3<span class=3D"cit=
e-bracket">]</span></a></sup> Since then, many websites offer web APIs for =
people to access their public database.
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id=3D"Techniques">Techniques</h2>=
<span class=3D"mw-editsection"><span class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">[</sp=
an><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DWeb_scraping&amp=
;action=3Dedit&amp;section=3D2" title=3D"Edit section: Techniques"><span>ed=
it</span></a><span class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<link rel=3D"mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href=3D"mw-data:TemplateStyles:r=
1305433154"><table class=3D"box-Howto plainlinks metadata ambox ambox-conte=
nt" role=3D"presentation"><tbody><tr><td class=3D"mbox-image"><div class=3D=
"mbox-image-div"><span typeof=3D"mw:File"><span><img alt=3D"" src=3D"https:=
//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/b/b4/Ambox_important.svg/40px-Amb=
ox_important.svg.png" decoding=3D"async" width=3D"40" height=3D"40" class=
=3D"mw-file-element" data-file-width=3D"40" data-file-height=3D"40"></span>=
</span></div></td><td class=3D"mbox-text"><div class=3D"mbox-text-span">Thi=
s section <b>contains <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Wh=
at_Wikipedia_is_not#GUIDE" title=3D"Wikipedia:What Wikipedia is not">instru=
ctions or advice</a></b>.<span class=3D"hide-when-compact"> Wikipedia is no=
t a guidebook; please help <a class=3D"external text" href=3D"https://en.wi=
kipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DWeb_scraping&amp;action=3Dedit">rewrite suc=
h content</a> to be encyclopedic or move it to <a href=3D"https://en.wikive=
rsity.org/wiki/" class=3D"extiw" title=3D"v:">Wikiversity</a>, <a href=3D"h=
ttps://en.wikibooks.org/wiki/" class=3D"extiw" title=3D"b:">Wikibooks</a>, =
or <a href=3D"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/" class=3D"extiw" title=3D"voy=
:">Wikivoyage</a>.</span>  <span class=3D"date-container"><i>(<span class=
=3D"date">October 2025</span>)</i></span></div></td></tr></tbody></table>
<p>Data extraction techniques range from manual collection to sophisticated=
 automated systems. Advanced methods analyze the underlying structure of we=
b pages to transform unstructured content into a <a href=3D"https://en.wiki=
pedia.org/wiki/Machine-readable_medium_and_data" title=3D"Machine-readable =
medium and data">machine-readable format</a>. These techniques utilize <a h=
ref=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Text_processing" title=3D"Text process=
ing">text processing</a> or <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artifi=
cial_intelligence" title=3D"Artificial intelligence">artificial intelligenc=
e</a>, aligning with the technical goals of the <a href=3D"https://en.wikip=
edia.org/wiki/Semantic_Web" title=3D"Semantic Web">Semantic Web</a>.
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id=3D"Human_copy-and-paste">Human=
 copy-and-paste</h3><span class=3D"mw-editsection"><span class=3D"mw-editse=
ction-bracket">[</span><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?titl=
e=3DWeb_scraping&amp;action=3Dedit&amp;section=3D3" title=3D"Edit section: =
Human copy-and-paste"><span>edit</span></a><span class=3D"mw-editsection-br=
acket">]</span></span></div>
<p>The simplest form of web scraping is manual copying and pasting of data =
from a web page into a text file or spreadsheet. This approach requires no =
technical tools and can be used when automated scraping is blocked by websi=
te restrictions or when human judgment is necessary to interpret complex co=
ntent. However, manual scraping is highly inefficient for large datasets, a=
s it is time-consuming, prone to human error, and mentally exhausting. For =
this reason, it is generally considered impractical compared to automated m=
ethods, except in cases where automation is not feasible.
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id=3D"Text_pattern_matching">Text=
 pattern matching</h3><span class=3D"mw-editsection"><span class=3D"mw-edit=
section-bracket">[</span><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?ti=
tle=3DWeb_scraping&amp;action=3Dedit&amp;section=3D4" title=3D"Edit section=
: Text pattern matching"><span>edit</span></a><span class=3D"mw-editsection=
-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<p>A simple yet capable approach to extract information from web pages is t=
o use the UNIX <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grep" title=3D"Grep=
">grep</a> command or <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Regular_expr=
ession" title=3D"Regular expression">regular expression</a>-matching facili=
ties of programming languages (for instance <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia=
.org/wiki/Perl" title=3D"Perl">Perl</a> or <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.=
org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)" title=3D"Python (programming langua=
ge)">Python</a>), in order to find text matching a specified pattern.
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id=3D"HTTP_programming">HTTP prog=
ramming</h3><span class=3D"mw-editsection"><span class=3D"mw-editsection-br=
acket">[</span><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DWeb_=
scraping&amp;action=3Dedit&amp;section=3D5" title=3D"Edit section: HTTP pro=
gramming"><span>edit</span></a><span class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">]</sp=
an></span></div>
<p><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Static_web_page" title=3D"Stati=
c web page">Static</a> and <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dynamic=
_web_page" title=3D"Dynamic web page">dynamic web pages</a> can be retrieve=
d by posting HTTP requests to the remote web server using <a href=3D"https:=
//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Socket_programming" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"=
Socket programming">socket programming</a>.
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id=3D"HTML_parsing">HTML parsing<=
/h3><span class=3D"mw-editsection"><span class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">[=
</span><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DWeb_scraping=
&amp;action=3Dedit&amp;section=3D6" title=3D"Edit section: HTML parsing"><s=
pan>edit</span></a><span class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></=
div>
<p>Many websites have large collections of pages generated dynamically from=
 an underlying structured source like a database. Data of the same category=
 are typically encoded into similar pages by a common script or template. I=
n data mining, a program that detects such templates in a particular inform=
ation source, extracts its content, and translates it into a relational for=
m, is called a <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wrapper_(data_minin=
g)" title=3D"Wrapper (data mining)">wrapper</a>. Wrapper generation algorit=
hms assume that input pages of a wrapper induction system conform to a comm=
on template and that they can be easily identified in terms of a URL common=
 scheme.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-4" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wi=
kipedia.org/wiki/Web_scraping#cite_note-4"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</=
span>4<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> Moreover, some <a hre=
f=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Semi-structured_data" title=3D"Semi-stru=
ctured data">semi-structured data</a> query languages, such as <a href=3D"h=
ttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/XQuery" title=3D"XQuery">XQuery</a> and the HT=
QL, can be used to parse HTML pages and to retrieve and transform page cont=
ent.
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id=3D"DOM_parsing">DOM parsing</h=
3><span class=3D"mw-editsection"><span class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">[</=
span><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DWeb_scraping&a=
mp;action=3Dedit&amp;section=3D7" title=3D"Edit section: DOM parsing"><span=
>edit</span></a><span class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div=
>
<link rel=3D"mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href=3D"mw-data:TemplateStyles:r=
1320445320"><div role=3D"note" class=3D"hatnote navigation-not-searchable">=
Further information: <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Document_Obje=
ct_Model" title=3D"Document Object Model">Document Object Model</a></div>
<p>By using a program such as <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sele=
nium_(software)" title=3D"Selenium (software)">Selenium</a> or <a href=3D"h=
ttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Playwright_(software)" title=3D"Playwright (so=
ftware)">Playwright</a>, developers can control a web browser such as <a hr=
ef=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ChromeOS" title=3D"ChromeOS">Chrome</a>=
 or <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Firefox" title=3D"Firefox">Fir=
efox</a> wherein they can load, navigate, and retrieve data from websites. =
This method can be especially useful for scraping data from dynamic sites s=
ince a web browser will fully load each page. Once an entire page is loaded=
, you can access and parse the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Doc=
ument_Object_Model" title=3D"Document Object Model">DOM</a> using an expres=
sion language such as <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/XPath" title=
=3D"XPath">XPath</a>.
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id=3D"Vertical_aggregation">Verti=
cal aggregation</h3><span class=3D"mw-editsection"><span class=3D"mw-editse=
ction-bracket">[</span><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?titl=
e=3DWeb_scraping&amp;action=3Dedit&amp;section=3D8" title=3D"Edit section: =
Vertical aggregation"><span>edit</span></a><span class=3D"mw-editsection-br=
acket">]</span></span></div>
<p>There are several companies that have developed vertical specific harves=
ting platforms. These platforms create and monitor a multitude of "bots" fo=
r specific verticals with no "man in the loop" (no direct human involvement=
), and no work related to a specific target site. The preparation involves =
establishing the knowledge base for the entire vertical and then the platfo=
rm creates the bots automatically. The platform's robustness is measured by=
 the quality of the information it retrieves (usually number of fields) and=
 its scalability (how quick it can scale up to hundreds or thousands of sit=
es). This scalability is mostly used to target the <a href=3D"https://en.wi=
kipedia.org/wiki/Long_Tail" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Long Tail">Long =
Tail</a> of sites that common aggregators find complicated or too labor-int=
ensive to harvest content from.
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id=3D"Semantic_annotation_recogni=
zing">Semantic annotation recognizing</h3><span class=3D"mw-editsection"><s=
pan class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedi=
a.org/w/index.php?title=3DWeb_scraping&amp;action=3Dedit&amp;section=3D9" t=
itle=3D"Edit section: Semantic annotation recognizing"><span>edit</span></a=
><span class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<p>The pages being scraped may embrace <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/=
wiki/Metadata" title=3D"Metadata">metadata</a> or semantic markups and anno=
tations, which can be used to locate specific data snippets. If the annotat=
ions are embedded in the pages, as <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki=
/Microformat" title=3D"Microformat">Microformat</a> does, this technique ca=
n be viewed as a special case of DOM parsing. In another case, the annotati=
ons, organized into a semantic layer,<sup id=3D"cite_ref-5" class=3D"refere=
nce"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Web_scraping#cite_note-5"><sp=
an class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>5<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a=
></sup> are stored and managed separately from the web pages, so the scrape=
rs can retrieve data schema and instructions from this layer before scrapin=
g the pages.
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id=3D"Computer_vision_web-page_an=
alysis">Computer vision web-page analysis</h3><span class=3D"mw-editsection=
"><span class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href=3D"https://en.wiki=
pedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DWeb_scraping&amp;action=3Dedit&amp;section=3D=
10" title=3D"Edit section: Computer vision web-page analysis"><span>edit</s=
pan></a><span class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<p>There are efforts using <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Machine=
_learning" title=3D"Machine learning">machine learning</a> and <a href=3D"h=
ttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Computer_vision" title=3D"Computer vision">com=
puter vision</a> that attempt to identify and extract information from web =
pages by interpreting pages visually as a human being might.<sup id=3D"cite=
_ref-6" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Web_sc=
raping#cite_note-6"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>6<span class=3D"ci=
te-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id=3D"Legal_issues">Legal issues<=
/h2><span class=3D"mw-editsection"><span class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">[=
</span><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DWeb_scraping=
&amp;action=3Dedit&amp;section=3D11" title=3D"Edit section: Legal issues"><=
span>edit</span></a><span class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span><=
/div>
<link rel=3D"mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href=3D"mw-data:TemplateStyles:r=
1305433154"><table class=3D"box-Globalize plainlinks metadata ambox ambox-c=
ontent ambox-globalize" role=3D"presentation"><tbody><tr><td class=3D"mbox-=
image"><div class=3D"mbox-image-div"><span typeof=3D"mw:File"><span><img al=
t=3D"Globe icon." src=3D"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thu=
mb/b/bd/Ambox_globe_content.svg/60px-Ambox_globe_content.svg.png" decoding=
=3D"async" width=3D"48" height=3D"40" class=3D"mw-file-element" data-file-w=
idth=3D"350" data-file-height=3D"290"></span></span></div></td><td class=3D=
"mbox-text"><div class=3D"mbox-text-span">The examples and perspective in t=
his section <b>deal primarily with the United States and do not represent a=
 <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:WikiProject_Countering_=
systemic_bias" title=3D"Wikipedia:WikiProject Countering systemic bias">wor=
ldwide view</a> of the subject</b>.<span class=3D"hide-when-compact"> You m=
ay <a class=3D"external text" href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?=
title=3DWeb_scraping&amp;action=3Dedit">improve this section</a>, discuss t=
he issue on the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Talk:Web_scraping"=
 title=3D"Talk:Web scraping">talk page</a>, or create a new section, as app=
ropriate.</span>  <span class=3D"date-container"><i>(<span class=3D"date">O=
ctober 2015</span>)</i></span><span class=3D"hide-when-compact"><i> (<small=
><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Help:Maintenance_template_removal=
" title=3D"Help:Maintenance template removal">Learn how and when to remove =
this message</a></small>)</i></span></div></td></tr></tbody></table>
<p>The legality of web scraping varies across the world. In general, web sc=
raping may be against the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Terms_of=
_service" title=3D"Terms of service">terms of service</a> of some websites,=
 but the enforceability of these terms is unclear.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-7" cl=
ass=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Web_scraping#cit=
e_note-7"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>7<span class=3D"cite-bracket=
">]</span></a></sup>
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id=3D"United_States">United State=
s</h3><span class=3D"mw-editsection"><span class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket"=
>[</span><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DWeb_scrapi=
ng&amp;action=3Dedit&amp;section=3D12" title=3D"Edit section: United States=
"><span>edit</span></a><span class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></spa=
n></div>
<p>In the United States, website owners can use three major <a href=3D"http=
s://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cause_of_action" title=3D"Cause of action">legal =
claims</a> to prevent undesired web scraping: (1) copyright infringement (c=
ompilation), (2) violation of the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/=
Computer_Fraud_and_Abuse_Act" title=3D"Computer Fraud and Abuse Act">Comput=
er Fraud and Abuse Act</a> ("CFAA"), and (3) <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedi=
a.org/wiki/Trespass_to_chattels" title=3D"Trespass to chattels">trespass to=
 chattel</a>.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-8" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://=
en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Web_scraping#cite_note-8"><span class=3D"cite-bracket=
">[</span>8<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> However, the eff=
ectiveness of these claims relies upon meeting various criteria, and the ca=
se law is still evolving. For example, with regard to copyright, while outr=
ight duplication of original expression will in many cases be illegal, in t=
he United States the courts ruled in <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wi=
ki/Feist_Publications,_Inc.,_v._Rural_Telephone_Service_Co." class=3D"mw-re=
direct" title=3D"Feist Publications, Inc., v. Rural Telephone Service Co.">=
<i>Feist Publications v. Rural Telephone Service</i></a> that duplication o=
f facts is allowable.
</p><p>U.S. courts have acknowledged that users of "scrapers" or "robots" m=
ay be held liable for committing <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/T=
respass_to_chattels" title=3D"Trespass to chattels">trespass to chattels</a=
>,<sup id=3D"cite_ref-9" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedi=
a.org/wiki/Web_scraping#cite_note-9"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>9=
<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup><sup id=3D"cite_ref-10" clas=
s=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Web_scraping#cite_=
note-10"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>10<span class=3D"cite-bracket=
">]</span></a></sup> which involves a computer system itself being consider=
ed personal property upon which the user of a scraper is trespassing. The b=
est known of these cases, <i><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/EBay_=
v._Bidder%27s_Edge" title=3D"EBay v. Bidder's Edge">eBay v. Bidder's Edge</=
a></i>, resulted in an injunction ordering Bidder's Edge to stop accessing,=
 collecting, and indexing auctions from the eBay web site. This case involv=
ed automatic placing of bids, known as <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/=
wiki/Auction_sniping" title=3D"Auction sniping">auction sniping</a>. Howeve=
r, in order to succeed on a claim of trespass to <a href=3D"https://en.wiki=
pedia.org/wiki/Personal_property" title=3D"Personal property">chattels</a>,=
 the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Plaintiff" title=3D"Plaintiff=
">plaintiff</a> must demonstrate that the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.o=
rg/wiki/Defendant" title=3D"Defendant">defendant</a> intentionally and with=
out authorization interfered with the plaintiff's possessory interest in th=
e computer system and that the defendant's unauthorized use caused damage t=
o the plaintiff. Not all cases of web spidering brought before the courts h=
ave been considered trespass to chattels.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-11" class=3D"r=
eference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Web_scraping#cite_note-1=
1"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>11<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</s=
pan></a></sup>
</p><p>One of the first major tests of <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/=
wiki/Screen_scraping" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Screen scraping">scree=
n scraping</a> involved <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/American_A=
irlines" title=3D"American Airlines">American Airlines</a> (AA), and a firm=
 called FareChase.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-12" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"ht=
tps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Web_scraping#cite_note-12"><span class=3D"cite-=
bracket">[</span>12<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> AA succe=
ssfully obtained an <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Injunction" ti=
tle=3D"Injunction">injunction</a> from a Texas trial court, stopping FareCh=
ase from selling software that enables users to compare online fares if the=
 software also searches AA's website. The airline argued that FareChase's w=
ebsearch software trespassed on AA's servers when it collected the publicly=
 available data. FareChase filed an appeal in March 2003. By June, FareChas=
e and AA agreed to settle and the appeal was dropped.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-13=
" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Web_scraping=
#cite_note-13"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>13<span class=3D"cite-b=
racket">]</span></a></sup>
</p><p><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Southwest_Airlines" title=
=3D"Southwest Airlines">Southwest Airlines</a> has also challenged screen-s=
craping practices, and has involved both FareChase and another firm, Outtas=
k, in a legal claim. Southwest Airlines charged that the screen-scraping is=
 Illegal since it is an example of "Computer Fraud and Abuse" and has led t=
o "Damage and Loss" and "Unauthorized Access" of Southwest's site. It also =
constitutes "Interference with Business Relations", "Trespass", and "Harmfu=
l Access by Computer". They also claimed that screen-scraping constitutes w=
hat is legally known as "Misappropriation and Unjust Enrichment", as well a=
s being a breach of the web site's user agreement. Outtask denied all these=
 claims, claiming that the prevailing law, in this case, should be <a href=
=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/US_Copyright_law" class=3D"mw-redirect" t=
itle=3D"US Copyright law">US Copyright law</a> and that under copyright, th=
e pieces of information being scraped would not be subject to copyright pro=
tection. Although the cases were never resolved in the <a href=3D"https://e=
n.wikipedia.org/wiki/Supreme_Court_of_the_United_States" title=3D"Supreme C=
ourt of the United States">Supreme Court of the United States</a>, FareChas=
e was eventually shuttered by parent company <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedi=
a.org/wiki/Yahoo!" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Yahoo!">Yahoo!</a>, and O=
uttask was purchased by travel expense company Concur.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-i=
mpervawp2011_14-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/=
wiki/Web_scraping#cite_note-impervawp2011-14"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">=
[</span>14<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
In 2012, a startup called 3Taps scraped classified housing ads from Craigsl=
ist. Craigslist sent 3Taps a cease-and-desist letter and blocked their IP a=
ddresses and later sued, in <i><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cra=
igslist_v._3Taps" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Craigslist v. 3Taps">Craig=
slist v. 3Taps</a></i>. The court held that the cease-and-desist letter and=
 IP blocking was sufficient for Craigslist to properly claim that 3Taps had=
 violated the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Computer_Fraud_and_A=
buse_Act" title=3D"Computer Fraud and Abuse Act">Computer Fraud and Abuse A=
ct</a> (CFAA).
</p><p>Although these are early scraping decisions, and the theories of lia=
bility are not uniform, it is difficult to ignore a pattern emerging that t=
he courts are prepared to protect proprietary content on commercial sites f=
rom uses which are undesirable to the owners of such sites. However, the de=
gree of protection for such content is not settled and will depend on the t=
ype of access made by the scraper, the amount of information accessed and c=
opied, the degree to which the access adversely affects the site owner's sy=
stem and the types and manner of prohibitions on such conduct.<sup id=3D"ci=
te_ref-15" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Web=
_scraping#cite_note-15"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>15<span class=
=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p><p>While the law in this area becomes more settled, entities contemplat=
ing using scraping programs to access a public web site should also conside=
r whether such action is authorized by reviewing the terms of use and other=
 terms or notices posted on or made available through the site. In <i><a hr=
ef=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cvent" title=3D"Cvent">Cvent Inc.</a> v=
. <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eventbrite" title=3D"Eventbrite"=
>Eventbrite Inc.</a></i> (2010), the United States <a href=3D"https://en.wi=
kipedia.org/wiki/United_States_District_Court_for_the_Eastern_District_of_V=
irginia" title=3D"United States District Court for the Eastern District of =
Virginia">district court for the eastern district of Virginia</a>, ruled th=
at the terms of use should be brought to the users' attention in order for =
a <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Browsewrap" title=3D"Browsewrap"=
>browsewrap</a> contract or license to be enforceable.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-1=
6" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Web_scrapin=
g#cite_note-16"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>16<span class=3D"cite-=
bracket">]</span></a></sup> In a 2014 case, filed in the <a href=3D"https:/=
/en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_District_Court_for_the_Eastern_Distric=
t_of_Pennsylvania" title=3D"United States District Court for the Eastern Di=
strict of Pennsylvania">United States District Court for the Eastern Distri=
ct of Pennsylvania</a>,<sup id=3D"cite_ref-17" class=3D"reference"><a href=
=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Web_scraping#cite_note-17"><span class=3D=
"cite-bracket">[</span>17<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> e-=
commerce site <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/QVC" title=3D"QVC">Q=
VC</a> objected to the Pinterest-like shopping aggregator Resultly's 'scrap=
ing of QVC's site for real-time pricing data. QVC alleges that Resultly "ex=
cessively crawled" QVC's retail site (allegedly sending 200-300 search requ=
ests to QVC's website per minute, sometimes to up to 36,000 requests per mi=
nute) which caused QVC's site to crash for two days, resulting in lost sale=
s for QVC.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-18" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en=
.wikipedia.org/wiki/Web_scraping#cite_note-18"><span class=3D"cite-bracket"=
>[</span>18<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> QVC's complaint =
alleges that the defendant disguised its web crawler to mask its source IP =
address and thus prevented QVC from quickly repairing the problem. This is =
a particularly interesting scraping case because QVC is seeking damages for=
 the unavailability of their website, which QVC claims was caused by Result=
ly.
</p><p>In the plaintiff's web site during the period of this trial, the ter=
ms of use link are displayed among all the links of the site, at the bottom=
 of the page as most sites on the internet. This ruling contradicts the Iri=
sh ruling described below. The court also rejected the plaintiff's argument=
 that the browse-wrap restrictions were enforceable in view of Virginia's a=
doption of the Uniform Computer Information Transactions Act (UCITA)=E2=80=
=94a uniform law that many believed was in favor on common browse-wrap cont=
racting practices.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-19" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"ht=
tps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Web_scraping#cite_note-19"><span class=3D"cite-=
bracket">[</span>19<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p><p>In <i><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Facebook,_Inc._v._Pow=
er_Ventures,_Inc." title=3D"Facebook, Inc. v. Power Ventures, Inc.">Faceboo=
k, Inc. v. Power Ventures, Inc.</a></i>, a district court ruled in 2012 tha=
t Power Ventures could not scrape Facebook pages on behalf of a Facebook us=
er. The case is on appeal, and the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki=
/Electronic_Frontier_Foundation" title=3D"Electronic Frontier Foundation">E=
lectronic Frontier Foundation</a> filed a brief in 2015 asking that it be o=
verturned.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-20" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en=
.wikipedia.org/wiki/Web_scraping#cite_note-20"><span class=3D"cite-bracket"=
>[</span>20<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup><sup id=3D"cite_r=
ef-21" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Web_scr=
aping#cite_note-21"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>21<span class=3D"c=
ite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> In <i><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w=
iki/Associated_Press_v._Meltwater_U.S._Holdings,_Inc." title=3D"Associated =
Press v. Meltwater U.S. Holdings, Inc.">Associated Press v. Meltwater U.S. =
Holdings, Inc.</a></i>, a court in the US held Meltwater liable for scrapin=
g and republishing news information from the Associated Press, but a court =
in the United Kingdom held in favor of Meltwater.
</p><p>The <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ninth_Circuit" class=3D=
"mw-redirect" title=3D"Ninth Circuit">Ninth Circuit</a> ruled in 2019 that =
web scraping did not violate the CFAA in <i><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia=
.org/wiki/HiQ_Labs_v._LinkedIn" title=3D"HiQ Labs v. LinkedIn">hiQ Labs v. =
LinkedIn</a></i>. The case was appealed to the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipe=
dia.org/wiki/United_States_Supreme_Court" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Un=
ited States Supreme Court">United States Supreme Court</a>, which returned =
the case to the Ninth Circuit to reconsider the case in light of the 2021 S=
upreme Court decision in <i><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Van_Bu=
ren_v._United_States" title=3D"Van Buren v. United States">Van Buren v. Uni=
ted States</a></i> which narrowed the applicability of the CFAA.<sup id=3D"=
cite_ref-22" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/W=
eb_scraping#cite_note-22"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>22<span clas=
s=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> On this review, the Ninth Circuit uph=
eld their prior decision.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-23" class=3D"reference"><a hre=
f=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Web_scraping#cite_note-23"><span class=
=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>23<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p><p><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Internet_Archive" title=3D"=
Internet Archive">Internet Archive</a> collects and distributes a significa=
nt number of publicly available web pages without being considered to be in=
 violation of copyright laws.<sup class=3D"noprint Inline-Template Template=
-Fact" style=3D"white-space:nowrap;">[<i><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.or=
g/wiki/Wikipedia:Citation_needed" title=3D"Wikipedia:Citation needed"><span=
 title=3D"This claim needs references to reliable sources. (April 2023)">ci=
tation needed</span></a></i>]</sup>
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id=3D"European_Union">European Un=
ion</h3><span class=3D"mw-editsection"><span class=3D"mw-editsection-bracke=
t">[</span><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DWeb_scra=
ping&amp;action=3Dedit&amp;section=3D13" title=3D"Edit section: European Un=
ion"><span>edit</span></a><span class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></=
span></div>
<p>In February 2006, the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maritime_=
and_Commercial_Court_(Denmark)" title=3D"Maritime and Commercial Court (Den=
mark)">Danish Maritime and Commercial Court</a> (Copenhagen) ruled that sys=
tematic crawling, indexing, and deep linking by portal site ofir.dk of real=
 estate site Home.dk does not conflict with Danish law or the database dire=
ctive of the European Union.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-24" class=3D"reference"><a =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Web_scraping#cite_note-24"><span clas=
s=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>24<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup=
>
</p><p>Ethical data scraping supports offmarket sourcing in business but mu=
st comply with GDPR to avoid privacy violations in automated data collectio=
n.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-25" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikiped=
ia.org/wiki/Web_scraping#cite_note-25"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span=
>25<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p><p>In a February 2010 case complicated by matters of jurisdiction, Irel=
and's High Court delivered a verdict that illustrates the <a href=3D"https:=
//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Inchoate_offense" title=3D"Inchoate offense">inchoa=
te</a> state of developing case law. In the case of <i>Ryanair Ltd v Billig=
fluege.de GmbH</i>, Ireland's High Court ruled <a href=3D"https://en.wikipe=
dia.org/wiki/Ryanair" title=3D"Ryanair">Ryanair's</a> "<a href=3D"https://e=
n.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clickwrap" title=3D"Clickwrap">click-wrap</a>" agreeme=
nt to be legally binding. In contrast to the findings of the United States =
District Court Eastern District of Virginia and those of the Danish Maritim=
e and Commercial Court, Justice <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mi=
chael_Hanna_(judge)" title=3D"Michael Hanna (judge)">Michael Hanna</a> rule=
d that the hyperlink to Ryanair's terms and conditions was plainly visible,=
 and that placing the onus on the user to agree to terms and conditions in =
order to gain access to online services is sufficient to comprise a contrac=
tual relationship.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-26" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"ht=
tps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Web_scraping#cite_note-26"><span class=3D"cite-=
bracket">[</span>26<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> The deci=
sion is under appeal in Ireland's Supreme Court.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-27" cla=
ss=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Web_scraping#cite=
_note-27"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>27<span class=3D"cite-bracke=
t">]</span></a></sup>
</p><p>On April 30, 2020, the French Data Protection Authority (CNIL) relea=
sed new guidelines on web scraping.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-28" class=3D"referen=
ce"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Web_scraping#cite_note-28"><sp=
an class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>28<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></=
a></sup> The CNIL guidelines made it clear that publicly available data is =
still personal data and cannot be repurposed without the knowledge of the p=
erson to whom that data belongs.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-29" class=3D"reference"=
><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Web_scraping#cite_note-29"><span =
class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>29<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a><=
/sup>
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id=3D"Australia">Australia</h3><s=
pan class=3D"mw-editsection"><span class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">[</span=
><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DWeb_scraping&amp;a=
ction=3Dedit&amp;section=3D14" title=3D"Edit section: Australia"><span>edit=
</span></a><span class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<p>In Australia, the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spam_Act_2003=
" title=3D"Spam Act 2003">Spam Act 2003</a> outlaws some forms of web harve=
sting, although this only applies to email addresses.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-30=
" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Web_scraping=
#cite_note-30"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>30<span class=3D"cite-b=
racket">]</span></a></sup><sup id=3D"cite_ref-31" class=3D"reference"><a hr=
ef=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Web_scraping#cite_note-31"><span class=
=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>31<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id=3D"India">India</h3><span clas=
s=3D"mw-editsection"><span class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href=
=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DWeb_scraping&amp;action=3D=
edit&amp;section=3D15" title=3D"Edit section: India"><span>edit</span></a><=
span class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<p>Leaving a few cases dealing with IPR infringement, Indian courts have no=
t expressly ruled on the legality of web scraping. However, since all commo=
n forms of electronic contracts are enforceable in India, violating the ter=
ms of use prohibiting data scraping will be a violation of the contract law=
. It will also violate the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Informa=
tion_Technology_Act,_2000#:~:text=3DFrom_Wikipedia,_the_free_encyclopedia_T=
he_Information_Technology,in_India_dealing_with_cybercrime_and_electronic_c=
ommerce." title=3D"Information Technology Act, 2000">Information Technology=
 Act, 2000</a>, which penalizes unauthorized access to a computer resource =
or extracting data from a computer resource.
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id=3D"Methods_to_prevent_web_scra=
ping">Methods to prevent web scraping</h2><span class=3D"mw-editsection"><s=
pan class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedi=
a.org/w/index.php?title=3DWeb_scraping&amp;action=3Dedit&amp;section=3D16" =
title=3D"Edit section: Methods to prevent web scraping"><span>edit</span></=
a><span class=3D"mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<p>The administrator of a website can use various measures to stop or slow =
a bot. Some techniques include:
</p>
<ul><li>Blocking an <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IP_address" ti=
tle=3D"IP address">IP address</a> either manually or based on criteria such=
 as <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Geolocation" class=3D"mw-redir=
ect" title=3D"Geolocation">geolocation</a> and <a href=3D"https://en.wikipe=
dia.org/wiki/DNSBL" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"DNSBL">DNSRBL</a>. This =
will also block all browsing from that address.</li>
<li>Disabling any <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Web_service" tit=
le=3D"Web service">web service</a> <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki=
/Application_programming_interface" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Applicat=
ion programming interface">API</a> that the website's system might expose.<=
/li>
<li>Bots sometimes declare who they are (using <a href=3D"https://en.wikipe=
dia.org/wiki/User_agent" title=3D"User agent">user agent</a> <a href=3D"htt=
ps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/String_(computer_science)" title=3D"String (comp=
uter science)">strings</a>) and can be blocked on that basis using <a href=
=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robots_exclusion_standard" class=3D"mw-re=
direct" title=3D"Robots exclusion standard">robots.txt</a>; '<a href=3D"htt=
ps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Googlebot" title=3D"Googlebot">googlebot</a>' is=
 an example. Other bots make no distinction between themselves and a human =
using a browser.</li>
<li>Bots can be blocked by monitoring excess traffic.</li>
<li>Bots can sometimes be blocked with tools to verify that it is a real pe=
rson accessing the site, like a <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CA=
PTCHA" title=3D"CAPTCHA">CAPTCHA</a>. Bots are sometimes coded to explicitl=
y break specific CAPTCHA patterns or may employ third-party services that u=
tilize human labor to read and respond in real-time to CAPTCHA challenges. =
They can be triggered because the bot is: 1) making too many requests in a =
short time, 2) using low-quality proxies, or 3) not covering the web scrape=
r=E2=80=99s fingerprint properly.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-32" class=3D"reference=
"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Web_scraping#cite_note-32"><span=
 class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>32<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a>=
</sup></li>
<li>Commercial anti-bot services: Companies offer anti-bot and anti-scrapin=
g services for websites. A few web <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki=
/Application_firewall" title=3D"Application firewall">application firewalls=
</a> have limited bot detection capabilities as well. However, many such so=
lutions are not very effective.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-33" class=3D"reference">=
<a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Web_scraping#cite_note-33"><span c=
lass=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>33<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></=
sup></li>
<li>Locating bots with a <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Honeypot_=
(computing)" title=3D"Honeypot (computing)">honeypot</a> or other method to=
 identify the IP addresses of automated crawlers.</li>
<li><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Obfuscation" title=3D"Obfuscat=
ion">Obfuscation</a> using <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CSS_spr=
ite" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"CSS sprite">CSS sprites</a> to display =
such data as telephone numbers or email addresses, at the cost of <a href=
=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Web_accessibility" title=3D"Web accessibi=
lity">accessibility</a> to <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Screen_=
reader" title=3D"Screen reader">screen reader</a> users.</li>
<li>Because bots rely on consistency in the front-end code of a target webs=
ite, adding small variations to the HTML/CSS surrounding important data and=
 navigation elements would require more human involvement in the initial se=
t up of a bot and if done effectively may render the target website too dif=
ficult to scrape due to the diminished ability to automate the scraping pro=
cess.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-34" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wiki=
pedia.org/wiki/Web_scraping#cite_note-34"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</s=
pan>34<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup></li>
<li>Websites can declare if crawling is allowed or not in the <a href=3D"ht=
tps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robots_exclusion_standard" class=3D"mw-redirect=
" title=3D"Robots exclusion standard">robots.txt</a> file and allow partial=
 access, limit the crawl rate, specify the optimal time to crawl and more.<=
/li>
<li>Trapping bots in a <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tarpit_(net=
working)" title=3D"Tarpit (networking)">tarpit</a>, feeding them nonsensica=
l data to <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Data_poisoning" class=3D=
"mw-redirect" title=3D"Data poisoning">poison their dataset</a>. This metho=
d is particularly effective against bots which ignore robots.txt files.<sup=
 id=3D"cite_ref-35" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org=
/wiki/Web_scraping#cite_note-35"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>35<sp=
an class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup></li>
<li>TLS Fingerprinting: Modern security systems analyze the <a href=3D"http=
s://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Transport_Layer_Security" title=3D"Transport Laye=
r Security">Transport Layer Security</a> (TLS) handshake to identify the cl=
ient application. Different clients (e.g., <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.=
org/wiki/Google_Chrome" title=3D"Google Chrome">Google Chrome</a> vs. a <a =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Python_(programming_language)" title=
=3D"Python (programming language)">Python</a> script) send <a href=3D"https=
://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cipher_suite" title=3D"Cipher suite">cryptographic=
 ciphers</a> and extensions in unique orders. This creates a unique "finger=
print" (such as a JA3 signature) that allows servers to detect and block au=
tomated scripts regardless of their <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wik=
i/IP_address" title=3D"IP address">IP address</a>.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-Sales=
forceJA3_36-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki=
/Web_scraping#cite_note-SalesforceJA3-36"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</s=
pan>36<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup><sup id=3D"cite_ref-Ha=
sDataTLS_37-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki=
/Web_scraping#cite_note-HasDataTLS-37"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span=
>37<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup></li></ul>
